# --- Real tax data must never be committed ---------------------------------
# work/ holds the filled-in personal CSVs: transactions, issuers, accounts,
# cash balances. out/ holds generated JSON, audit CSVs and threshold reports.
work/
out/
*_audit.csv
*_other_schedules.txt
*_threshold*.txt
*_threshold*.csv
schedule_fa_*.json

# The macOS Common Offline Utility test kit: synthetic inputs, the two JSON shapes to try
# importing, and the read-back of what they contain. All of it is regenerated by
# `python scripts/make_macos_import_test.py`, so none of it is worth tracking -- and the
# generated JSON would otherwise trip the schedule_fa_*.json rule below anyway.
macos-utility-test/

# --- Cached market and FX data ---------------------------------------------
# Regenerate with `itr-prep fx-update`; prices re-fetch on demand. Kept out of
# the repo both because it is derived data and because the cached tickers
# would disclose holdings.
data/prices/
data/sbi_ttbuy_usd.csv
*.cache/
.cache/
prices_override.csv

# --- The ITD JSON schema ----------------------------------------------------
# Not redistributed: it is the department's artefact, it is revised without notice, and a
# stale copy validating against the wrong year's rules is worse than none. See
# schemas/README.md for where to download it.
schemas/*
!schemas/README.md
ITR-2_*Main*.json

# --- The ITD Excel utility (large, copyrighted) and any broker export -------
*.xlsm
*.xls
*.xlsb
*.zip
*.xlsx
*.pdf
*.ods

# Typical download names, root-anchored so fixtures are unaffected.
/AIS*
/TIS*
/26AS*
/Form16*
/Form_16*
/*Statement*
/*statement*
/*Benefit*
/*Portfolio*
/*Holdings*
/*holdings*
/downloads/
/Downloads/
# Personal ITR data folders. Require a DIGIT after ITR so this can never match the
# itrprep/ package on macOS's case-insensitive filesystem (a bare /ITR*/ swallows
# itrprep/ and silently drops new module files from git -- that happened once already,
# 25 Aug 2026, to capgain.py / mf_input.py / cas_pdf.py).
/ITR[0-9]*/
/ITR[0-9]*.exe

# --- Credentials ------------------------------------------------------------
# .env holds document passwords, and the PAN and date of birth used to derive them.
# .env.example is the committed template and carries placeholders only.
#
# `*.env` is here because `.env` and `.env.*` between them missed the names people actually
# reach for: `secrets.env` and `creds.env` were not ignored at all, so `git add .` would have
# taken either one. The negation stays last so the template survives all three patterns.
.env
.env.*
*.env
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
credentials*
*_credentials*
# ...but not the suite whose whole job is to prove a credential cannot escape. It matched
# `*_credentials*` and was silently absent from the repository, so CI ran seven suites
# while the README claimed eight. The suite guard in .github/workflows/tests.yml now
# fails the build if that happens again.
!tests/test_unlock_credentials.py
.netrc
.git-credentials

# --- Editor / OS ------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# --- Keep the synthetic fixtures: they are how the input format is documented.
!tests/synthetic/*.csv
!tests/synthetic/broker_exports/*.csv
!tests/synthetic_split/*.csv

# Modal-dialog watcher artefacts. These normally land in the Windows workdir
# (default C:\temp\itrprep), but guard the repo in case --workdir points here.
*_watcher_up.txt
*_watcher.log
